declare(strict_types=1);
// Generated from the owner-supplied handbook; see scripts/precis/import-handbook.py.
return json_decode(<<<'HANDBOOK'
{"title":"MASTER PRÉCIS WRITING STEP-BY-STEP HANDBOOK","source_sha256":"e1b36b54b25edc343a460eb73fec43e63d68210f5044e70727a72fbe1c071561","chapters":[{"id":"1","title":"1. What a Précis Really Is","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"A précis is a faithful, compact restatement of a longer passage. It preserves the writer’s central argument and the essential relationships among the major ideas, while removing material that is illustrative, repetitive, decorative, or secondary. The aim is not merely to make the passage shorter. The aim is to make its meaning smaller without making its meaning poorer."},{"type":"table","rows":[["MASTER DEFINITIONA good précis is the original argument in a smaller, clearer, self-contained form."]]},{"type":"heading","text":"Précis is not ordinary summary"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A casual summary may mention only what seems important to the reader. A précis is stricter. It must preserve the writer’s line of thought. If the original passage argues that economic growth is useful only when its gains are broadly shared, a précis that says only “economic growth is important” is inaccurate because it removes the writer’s qualification."},{"type":"table","rows":[["Vague summary","Précis-style statement"],["The writer talks about education and society.","Education strengthens society by developing informed and responsible citizens."],["Technology has many advantages and disadvantages.","Technology improves efficiency but can weaken human abilities when dependence becomes excessive."],["The passage says corruption is bad.","Corruption undermines governance and obstructs development."]]},{"type":"heading","text":"The four qualities of a strong précis"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Fidelity: it says what the writer says - no more and no less."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Compression: it reduces length by grouping and generalizing ideas."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Coherence: it reads as a connected paragraph rather than as notes."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Independence: it can be understood without consulting the original passage."}]},{"id":"2","title":"2. Official Examination Framework","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Follow the current official FPSC syllabus and the instructions printed in your question. The handbook describes a 20-mark allocation (15 for the précis and 5 for the title); this assertion is awaiting independent official-source verification and is not presented here as a verified exam rule."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"About one-third is a teaching convention. An explicit question length instruction takes priority. Preserve meaning, fidelity, concision, grammatical correctness and an appropriate title."}]},{"id":"3","title":"3. The Non-Negotiable Principles","blocks":[{"type":"table","rows":[["Preserve meaning","Never change the writer’s position merely to make the passage shorter."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Do not add knowledge","Facts, examples, statistics, references, or opinions absent from the passage do not belong in the précis."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Do not argue with the writer","A précis reports the argument; it does not approve, reject, criticize, or improve it."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Do not copy mechanically","Use your own concise wording except where a technical term cannot reasonably be replaced."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Keep essential relationships","Cause, contrast, condition, concession, sequence, and qualification often carry the real meaning."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Generalize carefully","Replace lists with categories only when the category fully covers the listed items."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Prefer clarity over display","Simple, accurate English is safer than ornamental vocabulary."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Write a self-contained paragraph","The précis should sound complete, not like scattered notes."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Title the argument, not merely the topic","“Technology” is usually weaker than “Responsible Use of Technology.”"]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Revise for distortion","The final check is not “Is it short?” but “Is the writer still saying the same thing?”"]]}]},{"id":"4","title":"4. Topic, Central Idea, Thesis and Supporting Ideas","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Topic"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The topic is the broad subject of the passage: education, democracy, technology, population, media, poverty, governance, or another general area. Identifying the topic is only the first step."},{"type":"heading","text":"Central idea"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The central idea is what the writer is actually saying about the topic. “Education” is a topic. “Education must develop critical thinking rather than merely provide credentials” is a central idea."},{"type":"heading","text":"Thesis or controlling claim"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In argumentative passages, the central idea often appears as a controlling claim: the proposition that organizes the rest of the passage. It may appear in the opening, the conclusion, the middle, or it may be implied across several sentences."},{"type":"heading","text":"Supporting ideas"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Supporting ideas explain, qualify, prove, contrast with, cause, result from, or apply the central idea. A précis normally preserves the major supports but compresses their details."},{"type":"table","rows":[["ONE-SENTENCE TESTIf you had only one sentence to explain what the whole passage is trying to establish, what would you say? That answer is your first draft of the central idea."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Topic","Possible central idea"],["Topic: Democracy","Central idea: Elections alone cannot sustain democracy without strong institutions and informed citizens."],["Topic: Economic growth","Central idea: Growth is meaningful only when it improves the quality of life broadly."],["Topic: Science","Central idea: The value of scientific progress depends on how responsibly it is used."],["Topic: Urbanization","Central idea: Urban growth becomes beneficial when planning and infrastructure keep pace with expansion."]]}]},{"id":"5","title":"5. The Reading Method: From Passage to Skeleton","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Do not begin writing after the first reading. The most reliable method uses multiple readings, each with a different purpose."},{"type":"heading","text":"Reading 1 - Orientation"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Find the broad topic, tone, and general direction. Do not underline everything."},{"type":"heading","text":"Reading 2 - Argument"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Identify the central idea and the major supporting moves. Notice contrast words such as however, yet, although, while, but, therefore, because, unless, and only."},{"type":"heading","text":"Reading 3 - Structure"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Reduce each paragraph to a short label: problem, cause, effect, example, qualification, solution, conclusion, comparison, or warning."},{"type":"heading","text":"Reading 4 - Compression"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Mark lists, repetition, examples, quotations, anecdotes, and decorative language that can be generalized or removed."},{"type":"table","rows":[["SKELETON EXAMPLEP1: problem -> P2: causes -> P3: consequences -> P4: remedy. Once this skeleton is clear, the passage becomes much easier to compress."]]},{"type":"heading","text":"Signal words that reveal structure"},{"type":"table","rows":[["Signal","Function"],["however, but, yet, although, while","contrast / concession"],["because, since, owing to","cause"],["therefore, thus, consequently, hence","result"],["if, unless, provided that, when","condition"],["for example, for instance, such as","illustration"],["in other words, that is","restatement"],["first, next, finally","sequence"],["only, mainly, often, may, generally","qualification"]]}]},{"id":"6","title":"6. What to Keep and What to Remove","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"A précis is selective, but selection must be intelligent. The following table is a decision guide rather than a mechanical law."},{"type":"table","rows":[["Usually keep","Usually compress or remove"],["Central argument","Examples used only to illustrate"],["Major causes and effects","Repetition and restatement"],["Essential contrast","Anecdotes and stories"],["Necessary condition or qualification","Decorative metaphors and rhetorical flourishes"],["Important conclusion","Long lists of similar items"],["Major comparison","Rhetorical questions"],["Essential definition or technical distinction","Non-essential dates, names, and statistics"],["Logical connection between major ideas","Your opinion or outside knowledge"]]},{"type":"heading","text":"The deletion test"},{"type":"table","rows":[["QUESTIONIf I remove this detail, does the writer’s main argument change? If no, it is probably removable. If yes, keep its meaning - but compress its wording."]]},{"type":"heading","text":"When an example must survive"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Most examples disappear, but not every example is expendable. If the passage’s argument depends on a particular case, contrast, exception, historical event, or figure, the underlying fact may be essential. The test is functional: is the item merely illustrating the claim, or is it part of the claim?"}]},{"id":"7","title":"7. Conceptual Compression: The Core Skill","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The strongest précis writers do not shorten sentence by sentence. They move from details to categories. This is conceptual compression: several specific items are absorbed into one broader, accurate expression."},{"type":"heading","text":"Generalization ladder"},{"type":"table","rows":[["Detailed material","Compressed concept"],["schools + hospitals + transport + water supply","public services"],["roads + bridges + railways + dams","physical infrastructure"],["food + housing + healthcare + education","basic needs and essential services"],["slogans + propaganda + emotional speeches","emotional political appeals"],["loss of savings + borrowing + sacrificing necessities","financial insecurity"],["corruption + poor accountability + selective enforcement","governance failures"],["pollution + deforestation + resource depletion","environmental degradation"],["unemployment + low income + poverty","economic deprivation"]]},{"type":"heading","text":"Compression by merger"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Original: “Weak institutions allow corruption to increase. Corruption reduces public trust. Low trust discourages investment and slows development.”"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Compressed: “Weak institutions foster corruption, which undermines public trust and development.”"},{"type":"heading","text":"Compression by hierarchy"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Ask what higher-level category contains the details. If a passage lists universities, schools, and training institutes, “educational institutions” may be the correct category. If it lists housing, healthcare, education, and transport, “public services” may be more accurate. The broader term must cover all important items; otherwise it becomes distortion."},{"type":"heading","text":"Avoid over-compression"},{"type":"table","rows":[["Too vague","Compressed but meaningful"],["Corruption causes problems.","Corruption weakens governance and development."],["Technology is harmful.","Excessive dependence on technology can weaken human abilities."],["Growth is important.","Growth is meaningful when its benefits are widely shared."],["Democracy needs people.","Democracy requires informed and responsible citizens."]]}]},{"id":"8","title":"8. Paraphrasing Without Distorting Meaning","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Paraphrasing means expressing the same idea in a new form. In précis writing, good paraphrasing must achieve two goals at once: reduce dependence on the original wording and preserve the original meaning."},{"type":"heading","text":"Four safe paraphrasing moves"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Replace a phrase with a precise synonym: “places pressure on” -> “strains.”"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Change the grammatical form: “the failure of institutions” -> “institutions fail.”"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Combine parallel clauses: “reduces trust and discourages investment” -> “undermines confidence and investment.”"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Generalize a list: “schools, hospitals, housing” -> “essential public services.”"},{"type":"heading","text":"Do not paraphrase into a stronger claim"},{"type":"table","rows":[["Original qualification","Distorted paraphrase"],["Original: may weaken","Wrong: destroys"],["Original: often contributes","Wrong: always causes"],["Original: can improve","Wrong: guarantees"],["Original: depends partly on","Wrong: depends entirely on"]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["PRECISION RULEA shorter sentence is not a better précis sentence if it becomes more absolute, more emotional, or more general than the original."]]}]},{"id":"9","title":"9. Preserving Logic: Cause, Contrast, Condition and Qualification","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Many weak précis answers preserve nouns but lose relationships. Yet the relationship between ideas is often the argument itself. A précis must preserve the logical architecture."},{"type":"heading","text":"Cause and effect"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Original: “When institutions are weak, corruption expands, public trust falls, and investment declines.”"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Précis: “Weak institutions can foster corruption and undermine trust and investment.”"},{"type":"heading","text":"Contrast"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Original: “Technology improves efficiency; however, excessive dependence can weaken independent thought.”"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Précis: “Technology improves efficiency but may weaken independent thinking when overused.”"},{"type":"heading","text":"Condition"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Original: “Economic growth improves welfare only when its benefits are broadly shared.”"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Précis: “Economic growth benefits society when its gains are widely shared.”"},{"type":"heading","text":"Qualification"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Words such as may, can, often, usually, only, mainly, generally, unless, and when control the strength or limits of a claim. Removing them can change the author’s position."},{"type":"heading","text":"A practical rule for connectors"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Keep the logical function, not necessarily the exact connector."},{"type":"bullet","text":"“However” may become “but”; “therefore” may become a cause-result sentence; “although” may be converted into “despite” only if the grammar remains correct."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Never use “although ... but” together in the same construction."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Never use “despite + full clause” unless it is followed by “the fact that”; normally use “despite + noun/-ing” or “although + clause.”"}]},{"id":"10","title":"10. Building Coherence in the Précis","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"A précis should read like a miniature essay paragraph, not a collection of compressed notes. The reader should be able to follow the progression without the original passage."},{"type":"heading","text":"Three coherence tools"},{"type":"table","rows":[["Logical order","Preserve the original sequence unless a minor reordering is necessary for clarity and does not change emphasis."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Reference control","Pronouns such as it, they, this, and these must have unmistakable antecedents."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Economical connectors","Use but, however, therefore, while, because, when, and thus only where they clarify real relationships."]]},{"type":"heading","text":"Avoid sentence fragments"},{"type":"table","rows":[["Fragment / faulty link","Coherent form"],["Because weak institutions and corruption.","Weak institutions and corruption hinder development."],["However, the rising inequality.","However, inequality continues to rise."],["Despite economic growth but poverty remains.","Despite economic growth, poverty remains."],["Technology useful. Yet dangerous.","Technology is useful but can become harmful when overused."]]}]},{"id":"11","title":"11. Grammar, Tense, Person, Voice and Pronouns","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Tense"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Do not mechanically force every passage into one tense. Preserve the time relationship of the original while using the simplest correct tense. General truths are often naturally expressed in the present tense; historical sequences may require past tense."},{"type":"heading","text":"Person"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Avoid unnecessary “the author says” framing. State the ideas directly. If the original passage contains first-person material that is not essential to the argument, it can often be converted into an impersonal or third-person form; however, do not alter perspective if doing so changes meaning."},{"type":"heading","text":"Voice"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Active voice is often shorter and clearer: “Corruption weakens institutions” is usually stronger than “Institutions are weakened by corruption.” Passive voice remains useful when the receiver of the action is more important than the actor."},{"type":"heading","text":"Pronouns"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Use pronouns only when the reference is obvious. A short précis can become confusing quickly if “it,” “this,” or “they” could refer to more than one noun."},{"type":"heading","text":"High-frequency grammar traps"},{"type":"table","rows":[["Faulty / risky","Correct / natural"],["can intensifies","can intensify"],["depends of","depends on"],["invest on","invest in"],["leads towards progress","leads to progress"],["a real progress","real progress"],["people’s opportunities / peoples’ opportunities","people’s opportunities"],["economic opportunities is","economic opportunities are"],["media strengthens (formal plural sense)","media strengthen"],["although ... but","although ... , [no but]"],["the country may unemployed","the country may face unemployment"]]}]},{"id":"12","title":"12. Writing the Title","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The title should capture the controlling idea of the passage in the fewest meaningful words. A strong title is neither so broad that it could fit hundreds of passages nor so narrow that it reflects only one supporting detail."},{"type":"heading","text":"Title formulae"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Topic + relationship: “Technology and Human Dependence”"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Problem + condition: “The Limits of Economic Growth”"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Cause/effect: “Political Instability and National Development”"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Balanced contrast: “Freedom with Responsibility”"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Central principle: “The Need for Inclusive Development”"},{"type":"heading","text":"Title test"},{"type":"table","rows":[["THREE QUESTIONSDoes the title cover the whole passage? Does it reflect the central idea rather than a detail? Could the same title fit almost any passage on the topic? If yes to the last question, make it more specific."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Too broad","More precise"],["Technology","Responsible Use of Technology"],["Education","Education Beyond Employment"],["Democracy","Elections and Democratic Stability"],["Growth","Inclusive Economic Growth"],["Freedom","Freedom with Responsibility"]]}]},{"id":"13","title":"13. Word Count and Compression Control","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Many précis traditions use roughly one-third of the original length as a training benchmark. Treat this as a practical convention unless the question itself specifies a length. The real objective is disciplined compression without loss of essential meaning."},{"type":"heading","text":"Why exact arithmetic can mislead"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A passage may contain unusually dense argument, many examples, quotations, or repetition. Mechanical reduction can produce either a bloated précis or an inaccurate one. First preserve the argument; then tighten the wording."},{"type":"heading","text":"A practical length-control method"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Count or estimate the original length only after understanding the passage."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Aim for substantial compression during the first draft rather than chasing an exact number sentence by sentence."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In revision, remove duplicated meaning, inflated phrases, unnecessary examples, and weak connectors."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"If the question gives a specific limit, obey it exactly enough to show control."},{"type":"heading","text":"Word-saving substitutions"},{"type":"table","rows":[["Wordy","Concise"],["due to the fact that","because"],["in order to","to"],["has the ability to","can"],["a large number of","many"],["at this point in time","now"],["in the event that","if"],["for the purpose of","for / to"],["is able to","can"],["plays an important role in","supports / strengthens / shapes"],["results in the creation of","creates"]]}]},{"id":"14","title":"14. The Complete Step-by-Step Method","blocks":[{"type":"table","rows":[["THE MASTER SEQUENCEREAD -> IDENTIFY -> SKELETON -> SELECT -> GENERALIZE -> DRAFT -> TITLE -> COUNT -> REVISE"]]},{"type":"heading","text":"Step 1 - Read for orientation"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Identify the topic, tone, and general direction. Do not write yet."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step 2 - State the central idea"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Write one rough sentence that captures the whole passage."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step 3 - Build the skeleton"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Label each paragraph or idea-unit: claim, cause, contrast, example, effect, solution, conclusion."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step 4 - Mark essential ideas"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Keep the central claim, major supports, necessary contrasts, conditions, and conclusion."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step 5 - Mark removable material"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Examples, lists, repetition, anecdotes, decorative language, and secondary details."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step 6 - Generalize"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Replace groups of details with accurate categories."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step 7 - Draft from the skeleton"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Look away from the original as much as practical and write from your notes to reduce copying."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step 8 - Preserve logical links"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Ensure causes remain causes, contrasts remain contrasts, and qualifications remain qualifications."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step 9 - Write the title"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Base it on the controlling idea, not on the first noun in the passage."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step 10 - Revise for fidelity"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Compare your précis with the original. Check whether any important idea was lost or strengthened."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step 11 - Revise for language"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Correct grammar, pronouns, connectors, spelling, repetition, and awkward phrases."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step 12 - Revise for length"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Tighten wording without damaging completeness."}]},{"id":"15","title":"15. Rough-Work Templates","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Template A - Beginner"},{"type":"table","rows":[["Field","Notes"],["Topic","____________________________"],["Central idea","____________________________"],["Support 1","____________________________"],["Support 2","____________________________"],["Support 3","____________________________"],["Examples/details to remove","____________________________"],["Possible title","____________________________"]]},{"type":"heading","text":"Template B - Argument map"},{"type":"table","rows":[["MAPCLAIM -> REASON -> CONTRAST / LIMIT -> EFFECT -> CONCLUSION"]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Write only the nodes that actually appear. A descriptive passage may use a different map, such as situation -> problem -> impact -> remedy."},{"type":"heading","text":"Template C - Paragraph skeleton"},{"type":"bullet","text":"P1: ____________________"},{"type":"bullet","text":"P2: ____________________"},{"type":"bullet","text":"P3: ____________________"},{"type":"bullet","text":"P4: ____________________"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Overall argument: ____________________"},{"type":"heading","text":"Template D - Final fidelity check"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Have I added anything?"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Have I removed an essential contrast?"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Have I turned a possibility into a certainty?"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Have I preserved the conclusion?"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Can my précis stand alone?"}]},{"id":"16","title":"16. Common Failure Patterns and Their Cure","blocks":[{"type":"table","rows":[["Writing before understanding","Cure: force yourself to state the central idea and passage skeleton before drafting."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Copying sentences","Cure: draft from rough notes rather than staring continuously at the original."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Deleting every example blindly","Cure: decide whether the example is merely illustrative or essential to the claim."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Over-generalizing","Cure: keep at least the major dimension of the original argument."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Losing contrast","Cure: circle but/however/although/yet/while during reading."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Losing qualification","Cure: protect words such as may, can, only, often, generally, if, and unless."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Using inflated vocabulary","Cure: choose the shortest familiar word that preserves meaning."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Writing disconnected notes","Cure: use a clear subject, finite verb, and logical connector where necessary."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Repeating the same idea","Cure: merge sentences that perform the same function."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Giving a broad title","Cure: title the argument, not the subject area."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Adding advice or judgment","Cure: remove sentences beginning with “we should” unless that recommendation belongs to the writer."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["Grammar collapsing under pressure","Cure: prefer shorter sentence structures and revise verbs, articles, prepositions, and pronouns."]]}]},{"id":"17","title":"17. Expression Bank for Clear Précis Writing","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"These are not sentences to memorize blindly. They are patterns for expressing common logical relationships concisely."},{"type":"heading","text":"Cause and effect"},{"type":"bullet","text":"X contributes to Y."},{"type":"bullet","text":"X can lead to Y."},{"type":"bullet","text":"X undermines Y."},{"type":"bullet","text":"X strengthens Y."},{"type":"bullet","text":"X places pressure on Y."},{"type":"bullet","text":"X restricts access to Y."},{"type":"bullet","text":"X creates conditions for Y."},{"type":"bullet","text":"X perpetuates Y."},{"type":"heading","text":"Contrast and balance"},{"type":"bullet","text":"X improves A but may weaken B."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Although X offers benefits, excessive reliance on it can create problems."},{"type":"bullet","text":"X is beneficial when balanced with Y."},{"type":"bullet","text":"The challenge is to promote X without undermining Y."},{"type":"heading","text":"Condition"},{"type":"bullet","text":"X becomes effective when Y."},{"type":"bullet","text":"X is meaningful only when Y."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Without Y, X may remain limited."},{"type":"bullet","text":"X depends on the capacity to Y."},{"type":"heading","text":"Generalization"},{"type":"bullet","text":"These pressures strain essential services."},{"type":"bullet","text":"These weaknesses reflect broader governance failures."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Such conditions create financial insecurity."},{"type":"bullet","text":"These problems undermine social stability."},{"type":"bullet","text":"These measures support long-term development."},{"type":"heading","text":"Useful precise verbs"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"strengthen, weaken, undermine, restrict, expand, sustain, intensify, reduce, preserve, promote, hinder, foster, discourage, enable, constrain, erode, improve, threaten, reinforce, perpetuate, balance, regulate, protect, support, strain"}]},{"id":"18","title":"18. Worked Examples: Beginner to Advanced","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Open a worked passage in Full Practice. Identify its argument, write independently, then save a revision before choosing to compare with the handbook model. Models illustrate a method; they are not the only valid answers."}]},{"id":"19","title":"19. Self-Checking Rubric","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Use this rubric after every practice passage. Score yourself honestly before comparing with any model answer."},{"type":"table","rows":[["Criterion","Question","Score","Red flag"],["Central idea","Did I capture the whole argument?","/5","Too broad / too narrow"],["Fidelity","Did I preserve the writer’s meaning and strength of claim?","/5","Added or distorted claim"],["Selection","Did I keep major supports and remove minor detail?","/5","Examples dominate"],["Compression","Did I generalize and merge ideas effectively?","/5","Sentence-by-sentence shortening"],["Logic","Did I preserve cause, contrast, condition, and conclusion?","/5","Broken relationships"],["Coherence","Does it read as one connected paragraph?","/5","List-like writing"],["Language","Are grammar, spelling, and prepositions correct?","/5","Avoidable errors"],["Title","Does the title capture the controlling idea?","/5","One-word broad title"],["Independence","Can it stand alone?","/5","Depends on omitted context"],["Economy","Can any phrase be shortened without losing meaning?","/5","Wordy phrasing"]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Interpretation: 45-50 = strong control; 38-44 = good but needs polishing; 30-37 = meaning generally understood but compression/language inconsistent; below 30 = return to central idea, structure, and conceptual compression."}]},{"id":"20","title":"20. Practice Drills and Answer Key","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Drill A - Find the central idea"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A. Scientific research can improve health, agriculture, and industry, but research without ethical safeguards may also create serious risks. Progress is therefore most valuable when scientific freedom is matched by responsibility."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Central idea: ________________________________________________"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"B. A free press can expose wrongdoing and inform citizens, yet careless reporting can spread falsehoods and damage trust. Media freedom is strongest when accompanied by professional responsibility."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Central idea: ________________________________________________"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"C. Natural resources can support national prosperity, but poor governance may prevent wealth from improving ordinary lives. Effective and fair management matters more than possession alone."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Central idea: ________________________________________________"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"D. Education can create opportunity, but unequal access may reproduce social divisions. A fair system must extend meaningful learning opportunities across society."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Central idea: ________________________________________________"},{"type":"heading","text":"Drill B - Compress the list"},{"type":"bullet","text":"roads + bridges + railways + ports -> ____________________"},{"type":"bullet","text":"schools + universities + training centres -> ____________________"},{"type":"bullet","text":"food + shelter + healthcare + education -> ____________________"},{"type":"bullet","text":"corruption + weak accountability + selective enforcement -> ____________________"},{"type":"bullet","text":"loss of savings + debt + inability to afford necessities -> ____________________"},{"type":"bullet","text":"slogans + propaganda + personality worship -> ____________________"},{"type":"heading","text":"Drill C - Preserve the qualification"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Rewrite concisely: Growth can reduce poverty when employment expands broadly."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Rewrite concisely: Technology may weaken judgment when dependence becomes excessive."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Rewrite concisely: Political stability often encourages investment but does not guarantee development."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Rewrite concisely: Freedom is valuable only when exercised with responsibility."},{"type":"heading","text":"Drill D - Two-sentence précis"},{"type":"table","rows":[["PASSAGEPublic institutions gain legitimacy when citizens believe rules are applied fairly. Selective enforcement, corruption, and political interference weaken that confidence. Once trust declines, even good policies may face resistance. Institutional credibility therefore depends not only on formal authority but also on impartial and consistent conduct."]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Your précis: ________________________________________________________________________________________________"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"___________________________________________________________________________________________________________"}]},{"id":"21","title":"21. 30-Day Mastery Routine","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"This routine separates skill-building from full-paper pressure. Do not rush to difficult passages before you can reliably identify central ideas and compress detail."},{"type":"table","rows":[["Period","Main task"],["Days 1-5","Central idea only: 5 short passages daily. Explain why each central idea is neither too broad nor too narrow."],["Days 6-10","Keep/remove practice: mark essential ideas, examples, repetition, contrasts, and qualifications."],["Days 11-15","Conceptual compression: turn lists and multi-sentence cause chains into broader expressions."],["Days 16-20","Paragraph précis: 100-180 word passages; write one compact paragraph and title."],["Days 21-25","Mixed difficulty: abstract passages, implied central ideas, and arguments with qualification."],["Days 26-28","Timed précis practice with full revision and self-scoring rubric."],["Days 29-30","Full simulation, error log, and targeted correction of recurring language weaknesses."]]},{"type":"heading","text":"Daily error log"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Maintain four columns: Original mistake | Correct form | Rule | New sentence. Repeated errors should be reviewed every third day. The purpose is to stop the same mistake from surviving into later practice."}]},{"id":"22","title":"22. Final Examination Checklist","blocks":[{"type":"table","rows":[["BEFORE WRITINGI know the topic. I know the central idea. I know the passage skeleton. I know which details are examples and which are essential."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["WHILE WRITINGI am writing from the argument, not copying sentence by sentence. I am preserving contrast, cause, condition, and qualification. I am using simple, direct English."]]},{"type":"table","rows":[["BEFORE FINISHINGI have not added outside knowledge. I have not changed a “may” into a “will.” My title reflects the whole argument. My grammar is clean. My précis can stand alone."]]},{"type":"heading","text":"The 60-second final scan"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Circle every verb mentally: subject-verb agreement correct?"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Check can/may/must + base verb."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Check prepositions: depend on, invest in, contribute to, result in, lead to."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Check connectors: no “although ... but”; no fragment after “however.”"},{"type":"bullet","text":"Check spelling of repeated high-risk words: environment, government, opportunities, beneficial, scientific, development."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Check pronouns: every it/they/this has a clear reference."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Check title: specific enough to capture the controlling idea."},{"type":"bullet","text":"Check fidelity: no new advice, facts, or exaggeration."},{"type":"table","rows":[["FINAL PRINCIPLEDo not try to impress the examiner with difficult English. Impress through control: accurate understanding, disciplined selection, concise expression, logical coherence, and clean grammar."]]}]},{"id":"23","title":"Appendix A. Compression Dictionary","blocks":[{"type":"table","rows":[["Detailed material","Possible umbrella expression"],["roads, bridges, railways, ports","physical infrastructure"],["schools, colleges, universities","educational institutions"],["hospitals, clinics, health centres","healthcare services"],["food, shelter, healthcare, education","basic needs / essential services"],["jobs, income, business opportunities","economic opportunities"],["pollution, deforestation, resource depletion","environmental degradation"],["crime, insecurity, violence","public insecurity"],["corruption, weak accountability, selective enforcement","governance failures"],["debt, lost savings, unaffordable necessities","financial insecurity"],["slogans, propaganda, emotional appeals","manipulative / emotional political influence"],["loneliness, anxiety, dissatisfaction","psychological or emotional distress"],["delays, inefficiency, unreliable services","administrative weakness"],["poverty, unemployment, low income","economic deprivation"],["inequality, exclusion, unequal access","social inequality / exclusion"],["training, education, skill development","human-capital development"],["rules, courts, enforcement, legal protections","legal and institutional framework"],["trust, legitimacy, public confidence","institutional credibility"],["debate, tolerance, compromise, consensus","democratic cooperation"],["research, invention, technical progress","innovation"],["planning, budgeting, implementation","administrative capacity"]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Important: choose an umbrella term only when it fully fits the passage. These are options, not automatic replacements."}]},{"id":"24","title":"Appendix B. Error-Correction Bank","blocks":[{"type":"table","rows":[["Less natural / incorrect","Clearer form"],["Good governance helps in progress of country.","Good governance helps a country progress."],["Institutions are damaged by corruption.","Corruption damages institutions."],["Free media strengthen democracy by delivering right news.","Free media strengthen democracy by providing accurate information."],["Education enlightens citizens of their rights.","Education makes citizens aware of their rights."],["Economic growth improves society by the creation of opportunities.","Economic growth improves society by creating opportunities."],["Political stability creates balanced opportunities.","Political stability creates a stable environment for growth."],["There is unequal economic opportunities prevail.","Unequal economic opportunities prevail. / There are unequal economic opportunities."],["A country develops where people have equal opportunities.","A country develops when people have equal opportunities."],["Without education, the long-term country’s progress will stop.","Without education, a country’s long-term progress may come to a halt."],["Although growth is important, but law matters too.","Although growth is important, the rule of law also matters."],["Social media has benefits but excessive use can bring negative effects.","Social media has benefits, but excessive use can have negative effects."],["One-sided growth can intensifies inequality.","Unequal growth can intensify inequality."],["Investing on better social conditions.","Investing in better social conditions."],["A country may be unemployed.","A country may face high unemployment."],["Surge in prices can reduce living standards.","Rising prices can lower living standards."],["Fragile law enforcement can surge crime rate.","Weak law enforcement can increase the crime rate."],["Political instability slows down state’s progress.","Political instability slows national development."],["Humans should not be so much dependent upon technology.","Humans should not become excessively dependent on technology."],["Despite technology has made life easier but...","Although technology has made life easier, ..."],["The result depends of its usage.","The result depends on how it is used."]]}]},{"id":"25","title":"Appendix C. Source Note","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Primary teaching source: the Master Précis Writing Step-by-Step Handbook supplied by Ali Hassan Sargana. Its examples and drills are instructional material, not authenticated past-paper questions."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Official rules must be checked against the current FPSC syllabus. The source handbook cites https://www.fpsc.gov.pk/Syllabuses?section=CSS+Syllabi and https://www.fpsc.gov.pk/assets/media/2024-10-22-01-45-26-Syllabus_for_CE-2016_and_onwards.pdf. Its marks assertion remains pending independent verification."}]}],"passages":[{"id":"example-1","label":"Simple contrast","text":"Money can provide comfort and convenience, but it cannot guarantee happiness. People with wealth may still experience loneliness or anxiety, while those with modest means may live contentedly through strong relationships and inner peace. Lasting happiness therefore depends on more than material possession.","hint":"Skeleton: money provides comfort -> wealth does not ensure happiness -> non-material factors matter.","model":"Money can provide material comfort, but lasting happiness also depends on non-material sources of well-being.","model_title":"Beyond Material Wealth","source":"Owner-supplied handbook, chapter 18, example 1","limit":null},{"id":"example-2","label":"List compression","text":"Rapid urban growth increases pressure on housing, transport, sanitation, schools, hospitals, and employment. When cities expand without adequate planning, congestion, pollution, inequality, and social tension may increase. Urbanization contributes to development only when infrastructure and public services keep pace with population growth.","hint":"Compression move: housing + transport + sanitation + schools + hospitals + employment -> infrastructure, services, and economic opportunities.","model":"Rapid urbanization strains infrastructure, public services, and economic opportunities. It supports development only when planning and institutional capacity keep pace with growth.","model_title":"Managing Urban Growth","source":"Owner-supplied handbook, chapter 18, example 2","limit":null},{"id":"example-3","label":"Cause chain","text":"Weak institutions often permit corruption to spread. Corruption reduces public confidence in government, makes public services less reliable, and discourages private investment. When trust and investment decline, economic development becomes more difficult. Institutional reform is therefore central to sustainable progress.","hint":"Skeleton: weak institutions -> corruption -> lower trust/services/investment -> weaker development -> reform needed.","model":"Weak institutions foster corruption, which erodes public trust, service quality, and investment, thereby hindering development. Sustainable progress therefore requires institutional reform.","model_title":"Institutions and Sustainable Development","source":"Owner-supplied handbook, chapter 18, example 3","limit":null},{"id":"example-4","label":"Qualification","text":"Economic growth can raise national income and create employment, yet it does not automatically improve the lives of all citizens. If the gains are concentrated among a small section of society, inequality may deepen despite rising output. Growth becomes socially meaningful when its benefits are broadly distributed and translated into better living conditions.","hint":"Danger: removing “when its benefits are broadly distributed” would distort the argument.","model":"Economic growth can increase income and employment, but it may deepen inequality when its gains are concentrated. Its social value depends on translating growth into broadly shared improvements in living conditions.","model_title":"The Need for Inclusive Growth","source":"Owner-supplied handbook, chapter 18, example 4","limit":null},{"id":"example-5","label":"Advanced mixed structure","text":"Modern communication technologies have connected people across great distances and made information instantly accessible. Yet the speed of digital communication often encourages immediate reaction rather than reflection, while algorithmic systems may repeatedly expose users to views they already hold. The problem is therefore not communication technology itself, but habits of use that can narrow judgment and weaken thoughtful public discussion. Digital literacy must include not only technical skill but also the ability to evaluate information critically and engage with disagreement responsibly.","hint":"Skeleton: benefit -> behavioural/algorithmic risk -> technology itself not the core problem -> broader digital literacy is remedy.","model":"Digital communication expands access and connection but can encourage unreflective and narrow engagement. The main challenge lies in how technology is used; digital literacy must therefore combine technical competence with critical evaluation and responsible engagement.","model_title":"Responsible Digital Literacy","source":"Owner-supplied handbook, chapter 18, example 5","limit":null},{"id":"drill-d","label":"Institutional credibility","text":"Public institutions gain legitimacy when citizens believe rules are applied fairly. Selective enforcement, corruption, and political interference weaken that confidence. Once trust declines, even good policies may face resistance. Institutional credibility therefore depends not only on formal authority but also on impartial and consistent conduct.","hint":"Map fair conduct → trust → legitimacy and policy effectiveness. Preserve the possibility in “may face resistance”.","model":"Public institutions derive legitimacy from fair and consistent conduct. Corruption, selective enforcement, and political interference erode trust, reducing the effectiveness of even sound policies.","model_title":"Institutional Credibility and Public Trust.","source":"Owner-supplied handbook, chapter 20, Drill D","limit":null}]}
HANDBOOK, true, 64, JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR);
